good morning everybody i'm michael naft  i represent this area  district a on the clark county  commission i'm pleased to be joined  by superintendent jarrah peggy wilson  is the principal here at cartwright from  zero fatalities andrew bennett  we're proud to be uh partnering with our  wonderful ccsd police  and i thank the chief for being here  along with metro who is always here for  us in in full force  i also want to thank trustee guzman  whose district we're in here in district  a  uh and most importantly i want to thank  all of you because without you this  message would not get through to our  parents to our  students who in this unusual time are  are heading back to school and as you  all know on monday and just a few days  from now  students will be heading back to school  elementary school  they're our smallest students they're  our youngest they're the ones we really  need to make sure know the rules of the  road the rules of the sidewalk how to  ride their bike and be safe on our  streets sidewalks and  getting to and from school we also want  to make sure as they ride the bus that  they're safe doing that  and that the drivers understand that  it's been over a year  since we've had students on our roads on  our sidewalks  so this is really going to be really  going to require  some change in behaviors and attitudes  and really an  understanding for those in our community  who don't have students going back to  school on monday  and might not be in that mindset we want  to make sure that all of them fully  understand the responsibility  that we share in this process it's going  to require some some changing of habits  as i mentioned  and to our drivers we want to remind  them of the rules  always drive attentively obey all school  zone traffic control  including reduced zone speeds uh  when approaching school zones and  crosswalks reduce your speed and be  prepared to stop  stop for pedestrians and bicyclists and  wait for them to cross completely  never pass a car that is stopped at a  crosswalk  and obey the three-foot rule which is  three feet from a bicyclist  that's a law here in the state of nevada  and never ever drive distracted or  impaired  and as you can see with our force here  our friends and law enforcement will be  out there to make sure that you don't  but we also know that the responsibility  is not just that of our drivers but that  of our pedestrians and the youngest ones  as i mentioned  to them we ask that you plan your route  stay visible  wear light and bright colors stay alert  put your phone down don't wear your  headphones  walk on sidewalks if no sidewalks are  available  ensure that there is safe distance  between yourself and passing vehicles  and walk facing traffic only cross when  it is absolutely safe to do so  and make eye contact with a driver don't  assume that they see you  uh out as you're walking in front of  their car  we're not here today because of this is  some exercise or some date on the  calendar or some hypothetical situation  it was just a few years ago in 2008 that  a student of cartwright elementary  school  a nine-year-old boy was killed while  simply trying to leave school  he's been remembered as an energetic cub  scout who loved being outdoors  the driver was not impaired she wasn't  speeding she stopped at a stop sign and  looked left before turning right  hitting the boy in the process this  is an example of something that can  happen to any one of us in our community  and it's an all too painful reminder  that what we're asking for here today  has real life consequences  i'm begging our community please slow  down  please pay attention and together we can  make the return to school a safe one for  all of our students for our parents and  for all of the families  who are already dealing with so much i'm  pleased as i mentioned to be joined by  superintendent jara  who i'll turn the podium over to now  thank you very much  thank you thank you commissioner and  again thank you for being here this is  important um as you heard commissioner  nath um welcoming our most precious  asset  in this community our children back to  school is important so i  i i applaud the county for calling this  event  and really sharing some of the facts and  the need  for our community to slow down as we  start  to bring our children back to school on  a face-to-face  hybrid model as as has been noted  multiple times and discussed march 1  monday morning  with great anticipation and excitement  that our kids are returning to school  our educators are excited  in our schools and our classrooms as we  continue and move into a face-to-face  instruction for  all our children april 6th we'll  continue we'll have  all elementary school students  practicing social distancing and our  high schools  and middle school students will start  march 22nd  let me give you some facts so we're all  aware what's going on  in addition to our walking routes  and on our sidewalk we will be operating  1600 bus routes  across the valley carrying all students  we ask you to watch out  not only as our walkers are coming back  but to watch and follow our bus routes  and  our really are  our rules of operations when our when  our buses  are stopping and picking up our our  students  our police officers along with our  partners in metro  and all throughout the valley again  thank you for making sure that our kids  are safe  let me tell you a little bit about some  of the procedures that are also  important for the families to have a  clear understanding and the training  that our bus drivers  have gone through in our process to  cleaning our schools  and our buses students are required to  wear their face mask  at all times use hand sanitizers when  they're getting on  and off the bus the buses will be  equipped with self-dispensing sanitizer  bottles  and spare face masks in the event the  studer a student requires  a replacement face covering drivers will  load the bus  from the back to the front and unload  from the front to the back  each student will sit in the next  available seat and follow  seat markings to ensure that our kids  are practicing social distancing while  on the buses  students mo only report to the bus stop  on their assigned cohort  if you recall cohort a cohort b  between school bus routes bus drivers  and aides will use  disinfecting wipes and handrail door  frames and  on tops of the seats a thorough  disinfection process  will also take place each night to make  sure that  all the interior bus coverings are  disinfected again if you want to learn  more  about the procedures that we're taking  on our buses  reconnect ccsd.net has all the outlines  in there  now as we move forward i'd like  to introduce and welcome zero fatalities  program manager  andrew bennett who is here working with  all law enforcement including  our ccsd police  good morning my name is andrew bennett  a-n-d-r-e-w-b-e-n-n-e-t-t  i have the privilege of representing the  nevada office of traffic safety which is  a division  of the nevada department of public  safety and to manage our state's traffic  safety education awareness program  zero fatalities i want to start by  thanking commissioner naft and dr  jarrah for working with us on this vital  important public safety concern which is  making sure that our most vulnerable  road users  are safe when they return to school on  monday and throughout the rest of the  school year  there's a sense of urgency in our voices  when we talk about the importance of  pedestrian safety  as the last time that we had instruction  learning in the schools  which was august 2019 through march of  2020  we had 41 school children hit on their  way to or from school  one of those resulted in a fatality and  three of those were left serious injury  injur seriously injured which resulted  in life-changing injuries  and so when we talk about the importance  of pedestrian safety  literally lives are on the line and that  is the reason that we are here to talk  today about pedestrian safety  and school zone safety the mission of  the nevada office of traffic safety is  to eliminate serious injuries and  fatalities  so everyone arrives home safely and it's  that idea of everyone that idea of  perfection why our goal in the state of  nevada has and always will be  zero fatalities each year we declare  that our goal for the school year  is zero fatalities for school-aged  children and we fully intend to meet  that goal this year  as we have a abbreviated in-person  learning opportunity  and how we plan on achieving that is  through education and enforcement  this is the greatest opportunity that we  have through education so i appreciate  for everybody of the media being here  and we want to talk about two different  sets of laws number one  what is considered a school zone and  number two some of the common laws that  we want you to follow in that school  zone number one  for school zones when is a school zone  active there's three different ways to  know  if children are present if the lights  are on or 30 minutes before after school  those are all indicators that it is an  active school zone  the laws that we need you to make sure  that we're following as we approach  uh monday and throughout the rest of the  school year  commissioner nafta mentioned these  number one no u-turns in an active  school zone  number two no passing another vehicle in  a school zone  so what that means real quick is if the  speed limit is 15 miles an hour in that  school zone  and someone's going 10 or 11 miles an  hour the new speed limit is basically  going to be 10 or 11 miles an hour  you're not allowed to pass  another vehicle in an active school zone  and finally  speed speed kills we have extremely low  speed limits in the school zones and  that's for designed  to eliminate that possibility of a  fatality to happening  so we need to make sure that the public  is participating in those  slower school zones our office has a  program  called joining forces we partner with  over 27 different law enforcement  agencies  across our great state and we're using  that opportunity through a pedestrian  safety enforcement period  which is a maximum enforcement period  right now we're coordinating with all  the southern nevada law enforcement  agencies  including clark county school district  police las vegas metro north las vegas  highway patrol  henderson police department boulder city  to make sure that we have those  additional resources through overtime  funding  in our school zones as we approach the  return to school  on monday one of the things that we  cannot enforce  in school zones is kindness compassion  and understanding  usually we go through the school year or  we finish the school year we have all  summer  to get ready for a school zone with  school zones comes an increase in  traffic  increase in delays and a longer commute  but kindness and compassion is what  we're asking for  in addition to following the laws and  finally as a proud product of the clark  county education system here  there's a few names that will always  stick with me as i go  throughout my life miss uh miss yani  miss peebles miss heavy  um mr perry mr greene miss shumway miss  chanos miss  conklin those are the names of my  elementary school teachers when i went  to school here  we deserve it for them all their  children and all of us to make sure that  we're able to get them into the school  safely and transverse our school zone  safely  and so that concludes our press  conference we are all three available  for individual  uh press for interviews at this time so  feel free to grab us but we are not  going to take questions at this time  thank you so much
 

